6. Project Assumptions and Constraints 
6.1 Assumptions 
The project is based on the following assumptions: 
 The supplied datasets and supporting documentation will be available to the relevant 
team members. 
 Team members will understand their assigned responsibilities and provide progress 
updates. 
 The datasets will contain information relevant to the planned analysis, subject to 
verification. 
 The actual fields, completeness, consistency, and quality of each dataset will be assessed 
before detailed analysis activities are finalized. 
 Any missing, inconsistent, or unsuitable data will be documented by the relevant 
technical team rather than treated as accurate. 
 Team members will communicate challenges and delays promptly. 
 The project will use the tools and resources available to the internship team. 
6.2 Constraints 
The project will operate under the following constraints: 
 All required Week 3 deliverables must be submitted by the official deadline: Wednesday, 
11:59 PM WAT. 
 Access to specialist software, computing resources, and technical support may be limited. 
 Data quality issues, missing values, and inconsistent fields may affect the technical 
workstreams. 
 Delays in one workstream may affect activities that depend on its outputs. 
 Patient privacy and applicable data protection requirements must be maintained. 
 The project is an internship learning exercise and does not authorize access to live 
hospital systems or the use of the solution for actual clinical decisions. 
7. Project Acceptance Criteria 
The project will be considered ready for submission when the following conditions have been 
met: 
1. All eight required project management deliverables have been prepared and reviewed for 
completeness. 
2. The WBS clearly identifies project phases, activities, and deliverables. 
3. The project schedule identifies activities, estimated durations, dependencies, milestones, 
and responsible roles. 
4. The resource and budget plan identifies the required resources and estimated costs, with 
assumptions clearly documented. 
5. The risk register contains at least eight relevant risks, including their likelihood, impact, 
mitigation measures, assigned owners, and status. 
6. The quality management plan defines quality controls and acceptance criteria for the 
major project outputs. 
7. The simulated progress report includes at least two delayed activities and one change 
request, with their potential effects on schedule, cost, scope, and quality clearly 
explained. 
8. Corrective actions are proposed and relevant project records are updated to reflect the 
simulated changes. 
9. Technical workstream outputs are reviewed for completeness, with available testing and 
validation evidence documented. 
10. Final project documentation and handover materials are organized and ready for 
supervisor review. 
